On 2011-06-29 03:35, Jerome H. Fine wrote:
> In fact, is anyone still even running PDP-11 hardware and
> software these days - except for the few commercial sites
> which linger on since it is so expensive to change both the
> hardware and software.
>
> I enjoy running RT-11 using an emulator and adding a
> few enhancements to the software. One thing that an
> emulator provides are hardware combinations which
> never existed with DEC. For example, "terminals"
> are no longer restricted to 24 lines. This means that
> a lot more information can be displayed on each
> screen. And if I run a number of KEX sessions as
> systems jobs under multi-terminal support, then it
> is possible to substantially reduce the minimum low
> memory required when 6 system jobs are running
> which makes it possible to look at more different
> parts of a program file during debugging. Does
> anyone reading this still use RT-11 who might
> benefit?

[...]
Yes, Jerome. We know you run an emulator, is very happy with that, and 
very impressed by the performance of it, and so on. You have been saying 
that in almost every post for the last five years.

 > Did anyone ever produce terminals with more than
 > 35 lines which also had the ability to do what a
 > DEC VT100, VT220, etc. are able to do in just
 > 24 / 25 lines? The emulator allows up to 50 lines
 > using 80 character columns and 44 lines using
 > 132 character columns when the video card
 > supports these options in FULL SCREEN mode.

Speaking about terminals, yes, there have been terminals that can do 
more than 24 lines for a long time. The first one I ever saw was the 
Facit Twist, which could turn the display 90� if you wanted to. So 
either 24x80 (and 24x132), or else 72x80 (I can't remember for sure, but 
I think it could also do 72x132). And it was fully VT100-compatible.

Later, you had the DEC VT420, which could handle a page of 144 lines, 
even though all of it could not be displayed simultaneously, but you 
could pan around. It could show up to 48 lines on the screen though.

So yes, there is nothing new about having more than 24 lines, even on 
real terminals. And yes, also displaying more than one session on the 
same screen.

	Johnny

