glen herrmannsfeldt <gah@ugcs.caltech.edu> writes:
>> Turned out to be a real eye-opener.  Guess this stuff really has
>> no value anymore.  Makes you wonder why there are still companies
>> listing "re-furbished" (whatever that actually means) iDEC modules
>> for several hundred dollars.  Either they never sell any or they
>> actually don't even have any.  Good thing my garbage man takes
>> anything!!
>
> As far as I know, there are enough people still running old
> hardware that, with some probability slightly greater than zero,
> some of this will sell.   On the average, the sellers can
> (hope to) make money, even though most never sells.

The problem is that everyone naturally assumes that the value is in
"This object is a KDA-50."  But it's not, *being* a KDA-50 has zero
value.  The value is "I am a reputable electronic equipment dealer,
and if this KDA-50 does not function, I will get it working."  What is
being sold is a *service*, that service just happens to be attached to
one KDA-50 which happens to be lying around (of an effectively
infinite number).

This paradox is becoming incredibly common in the modern economy.  We
tend to think of physical objects as being inherently valuable, but in
many cases, they aren't.  It is the associated services.  E.g., a BIC
butane lighger, on its little blister-pack card, in a shipping
container on the dock in LA, costs about 1/4 cent.  You pay a dollar
for it in a store, almost all of which goes to keeping the store
around it, trucking it across the country, etc.  Which is why so many
*things* in life should be thrown away if they have no immediate use
-- saving it carefully for later would cost more than the thing is
worth.

Dale

