> think the biggest thing this shows is that there are far fewer PDP and
> VAX systems anywhere other than in hobbyists basements.

Speaking as one who knows where quite a few running VAXen and PDP-11s 
are still used in the real world, I can tell you that none of those 
places would ever go to eBay to get spare parts. There are used parts 
dealers still out there, and with that crowd you get the part you need 
directly when you ask for it, without bidding, loosing time, waiting for 
shipping, and possibly getting shafted. Not to mention the uncertain 
status of what happens if the thing don't work.

For commercial customers, the price of the hardware is not the issue. 
The real cost is sitting there waiting for the hardware to get fixed if 
it breaks. Compared to that, a premium price for a controller is nothing.
I helped a place a few years ago, whose RD52 had broken. Took a week to 
fix, because that disk was (unfortunately) their backup disk, used since 
their original disk had broken a number of years before that. That was a 
week where 120 people could not do their work. Guess if they cared what 
replacement hardware would cost? They switched to a SCSI controller on 
their 11/23, along with some SCSI disks. And yes, I did managed to 
recover all the data from the combined data extracted from both their 
broken disks.

But that crowd will basically *not* buy from eBay, and comparing the 
prices refurbishers charge with what you might get just don't work.

And the hobbyist crowd does not have lots of money to throw at things. 
Mostly I expect anyone getting something to just pay for the shipping. 
And that is enough. Basically, my time comes free, but I don't expect to 
actually hand out any money for giving something away.

So, the people who buy from eBay are mostly the clueless, who are 
perfectly fine with chopping up a working system into pieces, in order 
to maximize the money gotten, and who sell off the "good" stuff, and 
throw the rest away, not caring at all who the buyer is, if he even 
knows what he is buying, and don't care if the buyer gets upset if it 
don't work. Or any other number of variations of the "sell and run" 
principle. Grab the money, and to hell with all the rest.

Yes, I am disappointed.

	Johnny

