Respectfully -

Some things to think about:

My buying experience on EBAY is that if you see a listing from an
individual that says "No returns", you can bank on it being broken.

I will regularly bid on Heathkit hardware that is "No returns" but
I expect to repair it - and my bid is adjusted accordingly.

The amount of equipment on EBAY that states "bought this at an estate
sale and I'm too stupid/lazy to plug it in and turn it on to see if it
works - Condition Unknown, No Returns"- can't be true...

Very few sellers are honest enough to say that the equipment is broken.

My selling experience is similar.  If I have a nice piece of equipment
in working condition for which I want top dollar, you've got to state
that and you've got to stand behind that.  Guaranteed not DOA.

On the DEC side, as a hobbyist, my understanding is much more limited -
I wasn't a DEC service guy in a previous life.  Also, I'm never going
to pay what a commercial entity will pay for something.  On the other
hand I've been known to spend "real money" on occasions - I'm real
choosy - and I've got to be able to manage the risk.

When I buy, I'm looking for the seller to mentor me enough to get the
whatever-it-is working.  If the seller can't be bothered, then I risk
buying a paper weight.  Although it works - I can't make it work.
Sometimes during the auction I will ask a question just to see what
kind of an response I get.

I look at EBAY as a game.  It has written rules and unwritten rules.
If you know how to play the game you can do OK on EBAY.  It is not
bad or evil.  It is not even ideal.  It is what it is...

Rob







