Another potential problem is that the same source might yield different 
executables because they are linked with system libraries, and those 
libraries might have changed.

Comparing binary files is difficult because of the fact that binary 
files might be so many different things. For an executable for example, 
it might make more sense to report differences as related to load 
locations, and not file locations. But that requires understanding both 
the binary load format, and also understand overlay structures. And what 
if a difference is merely that the data is the same, but loaded at a 
slightly different address? That would mean all data are different, if 
you just look at the address, but in fact it's all the same except for 
the location.

And you can easily come of with a whole number of different 
problems/schemes/issues with other binary files. So there is no general 
meaningful binary file comparison. You need one tool for each type of 
file and type of comparison you want to do.
