Quite possibly true in general, but when considering the particular
case of RT11 executables, as we were here, I believe matters are a bit
simpler than the general case. My recollection of RT11 executables
(not that I've checked recently, though it is documented) is that the
format is *very* very simple, which makes the comparison simpler and
potentially more meaningful. My recollection says there is an image
code/data area and a relocation bitmap and not a great deal else; I
may well have the detail wrong, but the general principle of "it's
very simple" applied to much of RT11. And what is an overlay but some
subroutines and some IO?

Whether it's worth writing such a comparison tool is a different
question, especially when you add the possibility of disassembling the
RT11 executable using one of the various disassemblers on DECUS tapes
and the like. Disassessembling overlay calls might be "interesting".

[Background: I have spent far too much time at work writing and
understanding assemblers and linkers and object and executable related
things; not for PDP11 as target, but sometimes using PDP11 or VMS as
host, and/or PDP11 or VMS file formats].


