I assume that you are suggesting that the overlay
code for low memory overlays is the possible
cause.  Yes, it has changed a bit as well as the
length (by one word).

However, that is definitely NOT the problem.
First, I have a version of "ohandl.mac" which
produces the old code, so the overlay handler
portion of the code is the same as before.

Second, the root segments are different by 12 words::
(a)  TECO.SAV from V04.00 is smaller
(b)  TECO.SAV from the source code is larger but
      overlay segments 1 and 2 are smaller (by one
       word each) although overlay segment 4 is 73
       words longer.

Overlay segments 3, 5 and 6 are identical in size.

What is also apparent is that there are the same number
of entry points into each overlay segment:
(a)  7 entries for overlay segment 1
(b)  1 each for the other 5 overlay segments.

The above results were identified via a DUMP of each
TECO.SAV which also identified why (a) is 50 blocks
and (b) is 51 blocks.  Since overlay segment 4 required
1019 for (a) which is just 5 words less than 3 blocks
and 1092 words for (b) which is 68 words over 3 blocks,
it was overlay segment 4 which required the extra block.

Overall, there is much less difference between (a) and
(b) than I first assumed.  It may not take nearly as long
as I first estimated to change the source code for (b) to
arrive at the source code needed for (a).  The only
really unusual aspect is that location 50 for (a) is
MUCH larger than (b) or 43??? vs 33???.  Something
interesting is definitely increasing the initial size of
memory required to begin execution of TECO.SAV
in this case.  Also, my initial reaction is that (b) is a
version of TECO.SAV which is somewhat later than
(a) with maybe a bug or two corrected since in my
experience code usually grows rather than contracts.
But that can only be judged after the changes are made.

I am not sure if I have provided enough information for
Billy to be sure that I understand the problems.  If there
are any questions, please ask.

Jerome Fine

