By source code, I mean the MACRO-11 code commented source
files which are first assembled using MACRO.SAV, then linked using
LINK.SAV to produce the TECO.SAV file.

LINK.SAV does have a command file as well which specifies which
OBJ file is used for the root and which overlay segment.  Except for
the OBJ file for OHANDL.OBJ which is used for the overlay code,
it seems very unlikely based on the PSect list that any subroutines
from SYSLIB.OBJ were used.

>Sounds plausible to me.
>
>In principle a suitable comparison tool comparing the two executables
>could show up areas of common content (blocks of common code/data?) as
>well as areas where the files are different. Afaik the standard DEC
>file comparison tools aren't usable this way, because they like to
>compare lines of text not binary files such as executables. Same for
>the standard UNIX file comparison tools.
>
>That doesn't mean there isn't a binary file comparison tool that uses
>something like the well documented "longest common subsequence"
>algorithm on words (or even bytes) though. In fact for reasons I won't
>go into here I had to write something along those lines to compare
>text files while ignoring line boundaries - I don't own the code, my
>employer does, so I couldn't post it here even if it was directly
>relevant, but the comparison core wasn't that difficult, someone with
>sufficient motivation and expertise (and time) could do something
>similar.
>
>Interesting challenge.
>
VERY!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

While ASCII text files that have changes made to a small
number of lines can very easily be compared, I suspect that
it would be necessary to disassemble an executable program
to achieve something close to the same result.  Out of interest,
I did this about 10 years ago to a very short program of about
1000 words of code and data.  There were 6 versions for the
program which by then had seen many previous versions and
had become reasonably stable.  I added additional conditional
assembly code which allowed me to use one source file to
produce all six SAV files depending on which version I wanted.
Unfortunately, the original comments were still not available,
so some of the details are not clearly understood.

The key difficulty for RT-11 (and maybe for the other PDP-11
operating systems) is that most of the time the code and the data
are often mixed.  Thus data areas which differ by even one word
will pose an immense problem without a disassembly.  Even with
a disassembly, location labels which are not GLOBALs will no
longer be the same name.

My solution for making changes to the SAV files in RT-11 which
have a small number of changes is to first modify the source code
so that the mismatches in the number of words are eliminated.
This condition is easy to locate since prior to any such mismatch,
only offsets to locations higher than the mismatch appear in
isolated locations.  After the mismatch, almost every word in the
two SAV files differ.  Once the mismatches due to the incorrect
number of words have been fixed, the actual differences in the
code and date can be fixed.  However, this is a very labour
intensive manual procedure and can sometimes take more than
an hour per size mismatch plus additional time at the end to
correct any remaining differences, especially is the code was
written in a high level language.  Consequently, I would undertake
such a project only when it is absolutely required.

I know that I have not really answered your question.  My conclusion
is that such a comparison is reasonable only for ASCII text files
OR executables where a few isolated changes have been made and
the source code is available to be modified, i.e. the source code is
available (or can be provided) for one version and a second close
version also needs the source code.

Jerome Fine

