> Another potential problem is that the same source might yield 
> different executables because they are linked with system libraries, 
> and those libraries might have changed.

I doubt that this is a problem in this case.  TECO.SAV does
not seem to use any subroutines from the SYSLIB.OBJ file.

> Comparing binary files is difficult because of the fact that binary 
> files might be so many different things. For an executable for 
> example, it might make more sense to report differences as related to 
> load locations, and not file locations. But that requires 
> understanding both the binary load format, and also understand overlay 
> structures. And what if a difference is merely that the data is the 
> same, but loaded at a slightly different address? That would mean all 
> data are different, if you just look at the address, but in fact it's 
> all the same except for the location.

A big problem.

> And you can easily come of with a whole number of different 
> problems/schemes/issues with other binary files. So there is no 
> general meaningful binary file comparison. You need one tool for each 
> type of file and type of comparison you want to do.
> Text file comparison is actually just another special case of binary 
> file comparison, for one specific type of binary files, and one 
> specific type of comparisons.

While binary (or executable) files usually have references
to other locations in the file either as an absolute address
or as a difference (between the current location and the
other location), the most I have ever seen a text file reference
is a label (by name) in another part of the file.  For purposes
of comparison, this would probably never matter.  In addition,
text files are usually compared line by line.  Since the line
delimiter is always (at least in my experience) non-ASCII
text, it is rather simple for a program to isolate each separate
line independently of content and length.  So while a text
file may be a special case, the restrictions on the individual
characters which may be used allow individual lines of text
to be isolated - which can not be done for a binary file which
has no restrictions on the date information.

Jerome Fine

