In my experience, I don't really see that the mixed code and data in
an RT-11 SAV file will be easier to compare than separate
code (an execute ONLY area) and data (read / write ONLY)
areas in a VMS environment.  In fact, it might actually be easier.

>Whether it's worth writing such a comparison tool is a different
>question, especially when you add the possibility of disassembling the
>RT11 executable using one of the various disassemblers on DECUS tapes
>and the like. Disassessembling overlay calls might be "interesting".
>  
>
A disassembly is not trivial, even with an OBJ file
as UnMAC is an example.  The original version in
DECUS could not even UnMac itself.  It took a
reasonable effort to enhance UnMac to do so.
Of course, since the code / data was generated
by FORTRAN, it looked quite different from
MACRO-11 type programs.

As for SAV => MAC files, the only one I have
that handles an odd address .Ascii directive is
the enhanced version that I have for myself.  Even
then, it usually takes a few passes before I sort
out the code and data areas.

As for disassembling overlays, I started on the code,
but never finished it.  Too many other interesting
things to do.

>[Background: I have spent far too much time at work writing and
>understanding assemblers and linkers and object and executable related
>things; not for PDP11 as target, but sometimes using PDP11 or VMS as
>host, and/or PDP11 or VMS file formats].
>
I am still working on RT-11 systems as well.  Recently,
I wanted to reduce the size of low memory that the editor
program uses as a system job under a Mapped Monitor
(RT11XM) which has multi-terminal support.  Normally,
I use 4 DL ports to run 4 system jobs which are the KEX
editor as in:
SRUN  KEX.SAV/LEVEL:n/TERMINAL:n/NAME:Kn

The 801 words needed for each system job was causing
problems when I also used SDX.SYS to debug programs.
I found that as a system job, one of the data structures in
low memory was never needed.  The one time initialization
code in low memory was abandoned as well.  The solution
was to reduce the initial size of the stack, then modify the
stack address after initialization to also include the now
(about to be)  abandoned initialization code.  So I lowered
the base address to 600 and linked the overlay handler
into the addresses between 600 and 1000 ("/B:1000"),
then changed the stack address to 1000 before I called
the only overlay at virtual 20002 (well, the overlay
handler called the overlay.

At that point, the root section was one block less (1 instead
of 2 blocks).  Comparing the two SAV files required making
the comparison starting at block 2 on the original SAV file
and block 1 on the revised SAV file.  Fortunately, I have
a enhanced version of BINCOM which allows:
RUN  DU0:BINCOM
*DU0:FOOBAR.SAV/S:1.,DU0:FOOBAR.OLD/S:2.

There were only about 6 differences which pertained to
the size of the root - which no longer mattered.

So there is another problem when two different binary files
are compared.  If the start of one portion begins at a different
block, the files may compare, but there must be some easy
way of telling the program.  DEC did this for DUP via:
COPY/DEVICE   VM0:/START:25./END:34.  DU0:/START:155.
but not BINCOM.

I often do this when I want to copy a changed file over
the original location.  It requires being extremely careful!!

Jerome Fine

