When I used DECODE, I seem to remember that there was an
additional problem.  If there was an .ASCII reference which
started at an ODD address, it was unable to resolve the location.

I think that I added the ASCII directive which was able to support
ODD addresses.  I think that I also added an additional set of
symbols to use when BIT values were being used with the three
instructions (BIT, BIC, BIS) in order to distinguish those symbols
which started with a "V" used for immediate instructions - obviously
started those symbols with a "B".

To solve the SEVEN character symbol problem when the high
order bit was set, I just incremented the value of the symbol.
That produced SIX sets of symbols:
(a)  L00000 to L77776 and  M00000 to M77776
(b)  V00000 to V77777 and W00000 to W77777
(c)  B00000 to B77777 and C00000 to C77777

If anyone is interested, I will look around for the program.

Jerome Fine


