> usually means a reverse option, where the starting price is high and
> drops until someone agrees to buy. =A0I think the rules for the actual
> Dutch auction (traditionally and AFAIK still used when selling flower
> bulbs etc) are somewhat more complicated.

No, marktplaats is not conducting Dutch auctions. Marktplaats is not
an auction site per se, rather a
bootsale site. On eBay the auction process is time driven and when the
reserve is met by the bidder then
the item gets sold. At marktplaats bids are placed but whether they
are ever accepted is unclear. More often than
not a quick email of phonecall closed the deal. There is no obligation
to sell the item once a proper bid is placed,
like on eBay. Which makes marktplaats appear more chaotic than eBay.
The site was bought by eBay but that didn't
change the way it operates.

