Mine does as well, but there is a size limit which must fit
inside a standard plastic garbage bag.  For Qbus parts,
that does not pose a problem except for RL02 racks
and drives which have been passed to another home.
Fortunately, most of what is left is BA23 boxes and
5 1/4" hard drives plus Qbus boards.

I am curious.  Has anyone here purchased anything of
significance via ebay in the past few years for the PDP-11?
I know I see PDP-11 boards (mostly Qbus) for sale most
of the time.  And the prices are not out of line if the boards
are used in commercial sites.  But for hobby users who
rarely pay much more than shipping, the ebay prices do
seem far higher than is reasonable.

In fact, is anyone still even running PDP-11 hardware and
software these days - except for the few commercial sites
which linger on since it is so expensive to change both the
hardware and software.

I enjoy running RT-11 using an emulator and adding a
few enhancements to the software.  One thing that an
emulator provides are hardware combinations which
never existed with DEC.  For example, "terminals"
are no longer restricted to 24 lines.  This means that
a lot more information can be displayed on each
screen.  And if I run a number of KEX sessions as
systems jobs under multi-terminal support, then it
is possible to substantially reduce the minimum low
memory required when 6 system jobs are running
which makes it possible to look at more different
parts of a program file during debugging.  Does
anyone reading this still use RT-11 who might
benefit?

Did anyone ever produce terminals with more than
35 lines which also had the ability to do what a
DEC VT100, VT220, etc. are able to do in just
24 / 25 lines?  The emulator allows up to 50 lines
using 80 character columns and 44 lines using
132 character columns when the video card
supports these options in FULL  SCREEN mode.

There also seems to be something new in hardware
called a NuPDQ-1000 board.

As far as I can understand, an Intel CPU
(probably a core 2 duo) runs an emulator
program, possibly under an operating system
which runs on a standard Intel PC (maybe
even one of the Windows flavours).  What
I can't understand is why anyone would use
such an expensive solution as compared with
a standard PC running a PDP-11 emulator with
connections to a real DEC Qbus for those
boards which have not been emulated via
software and hardware with a PCI interface.

>http://www.transduction.com/pdf/NuPDPq_brochure_Transduction.pdf
>

Jerome Fine



