Mine does as well, but there is a size limit which must fit<br>
inside a standard plastic garbage bag.&nbsp; For Qbus parts,<br>
that does not pose a problem except for RL02 racks<br>
and drives which have been passed to another home.<br>
Fortunately, most of what is left is BA23 boxes and<br>
5 1/4" hard drives plus Qbus boards.<br>
<br>
I am curious.&nbsp; Has anyone here purchased anything of<br>
significance via ebay in the past few years for the PDP-11?<br>
I know I see PDP-11 boards (mostly Qbus) for sale most<br>
of the time.&nbsp; And the prices are not out of line if the boards<br>
are used in commercial sites.&nbsp; But for hobby users who<br>
rarely pay much more than shipping, the ebay prices do<br>
seem far higher than is reasonable.<br>
<br>
In fact, is anyone still even running PDP-11 hardware and<br>
software these days - except for the few commercial sites<br>
which linger on since it is so expensive to change both the<br>
hardware and software.<br>
<br>
I enjoy running RT-11 using an emulator and adding a<br>
few enhancements to the software.&nbsp; One thing that an<br>
emulator provides are hardware combinations which<br>
never existed with DEC.&nbsp; For example, "terminals"<br>
are no longer restricted to 24 lines.&nbsp; This means that<br>
a lot more information can be displayed on each<br>
screen.&nbsp; And if I run a number of KEX sessions as<br>
systems jobs under multi-terminal support, then it<br>
is possible to substantially reduce the minimum low<br>
memory required when 6 system jobs are running<br>
which makes it possible to look at more different<br>
parts of a program file during debugging.&nbsp; Does<br>
anyone reading this still use RT-11 who might<br>
benefit?<br>
<br>
Did anyone ever produce terminals with more than<br>
35 lines which also had the ability to do what a<br>
DEC VT100, VT220, etc. are able to do in just<br>
24 / 25 lines?&nbsp; The emulator allows up to 50 lines<br>
using 80 character columns and 44 lines using<br>
132 character columns when the video card<br>
supports these options in FULL&nbsp; SCREEN mode.<br>
<br>
There also seems to be something new in hardware<br>
called a NuPDQ-1000 board.<br>
<br>
As far as I can understand, an Intel CPU<br>
(probably a core 2 duo) runs an emulator<br>
program, possibly under an operating system<br>
which runs on a standard Intel PC (maybe<br>
even one of the Windows flavours).&nbsp; What<br>
I can't understand is why anyone would use<br>
such an expensive solution as compared with<br>
a standard PC running a PDP-11 emulator with<br>
connections to a real DEC Qbus for those<br>
boards which have not been emulated via<br>
software and hardware with a PCI interface.<br>
